NAME
====

**wattle** - run Janet programs and a Janet REPL

SYNOPSIS
========

**wattle** [_options_] [_script_ [_args_ ...]]
**wattle** [_options_] **-i** _image_ [_args_ ...]
**wattle** [_options_] **-c** _source_ [_output_]
**wattle** [**-h** | **-v**]

DESCRIPTION
===========

**wattle** runs programs written in the Janet programming language. It is a
Janet runtime written in Zig. With no _script_, and no option that does work of
its own, it starts a REPL (read-eval-print loop).

Options are processed in order, so an option applies to the options and the
_script_ that follow it. The first argument that does not begin with ``-`` is
the _script_, and every argument after it is passed to the script. Once the
script has run, **wattle** calls the ``main`` function it defines, if any, with
the script's path followed by _args_. The same list is bound to ``*args*``.

**wattle** exits when the script and any tasks it scheduled on the event loop
have finished.

Options
-------

- **-h**, **--help**:
  Print a usage summary and exit.

- **-v**, **--version**:
  Print the version of Wattle and exit.

- **-e**, **--eval** _code_:
  Evaluate _code_ as Janet source. The REPL is not started afterwards unless
  **-r** is also given.

- **-E**, **--expression** _code_ [_arguments_ ...]:
  Evaluate _code_ as the body of a ``short-fn`` and call it with every
  remaining argument as a string. No option after _code_ is processed. For
  example, ``wattle -E '(print $0)' 12`` prints ``12``.

- **-l**, **--library** _module_:
  Import _module_ into the environment before continuing, without a prefix, so
  its bindings are available to later options, the _script_ and the REPL.

- **-m**, **--syspath** _path_:
  Set ``*syspath*``, the directory system modules are loaded from, to _path_.
  This replaces the value taken from `WATTLE_PATH`.

- **-i**, **--image**:
  Treat _script_ as an image made by **-c** rather than as source, load it and
  call its ``main`` function.

- **-c**, **--compile** _source_ [_output_]:
  Load the module at _source_ and write an image of its environment to
  _output_. If _output_ is omitted, it is _source_ with ``.wattle`` replaced by
  ``.jimage``.

- **-k**, **--flycheck**:
  Compile _script_ without running it and report any errors. With no _script_,
  check standard input instead. Errors do not change the exit status.

- **-p**, **--persistent**:
  Do not exit from inside the loader when a script or a module loaded with
  **-l** raises a top-level error. The error propagates instead, and the
  process still exits with status 1.

- **-r**, **--repl**:
  Start the REPL after the _script_ or the other options have run.

- **-s**, **--stdin**:
  Read REPL input as raw lines from standard input and write prompts to
  standard output. Implies **-r**.

- **-q**, **--quiet**:
  Do not print the banner when the REPL starts.

- **-R**, **--noprofile**:
  Do not load the profile named by `WATTLE_PROFILE`.

- **-d**, **--debug**:
  Set ``*debug*`` and ``*redef*``. An error in the REPL enters the debugger,
  and a top-level definition can be rebound.

- **-n**, **--nocolor**:
  Print values, errors and documentation without ANSI colour.

- **-N**, **--color**:
  Print values, errors and documentation with ANSI colour.

- **-w**, **--lint-warn** _level_:
  Set the lint level at which a message is printed as a warning. _level_ is
  one of ``none``, ``relaxed``, ``normal``, ``strict`` or ``all``, written
  without a leading colon, or a number. The default is ``normal``.

- **-x**, **--lint-error** _level_:
  Set the lint level at which a message stops compilation with an error.
  _level_ takes the same values as for **-w**. The default is ``none``.

- **-b**, **--install** _directory_:
  Install the bundle in _directory_.

- **-B**, **--reinstall** _name_:
  Reinstall the installed bundle _name_.

- **-u**, **--uninstall** _name_:
  Uninstall the bundle _name_.

- **-U**, **--update-all**:
  Reinstall every installed bundle.

- **-P**, **--prune**:
  Uninstall every orphaned bundle: one marked for automatic removal that no
  other bundle depends on.

- **-L**, **--list**:
  List the installed bundles.

- **--**:
  Stop processing options. The next argument is the _script_ even if it begins
  with ``-``.

An unrecognised option prints the usage summary and exits with status 0.

REPL
====

The REPL reads a line at a time and evaluates each form once it is complete.
The prompt shows the line number and any open delimiters. Documentation for a
binding is shown with ``(doc symbol)``, and ``(doc)`` lists the bindings in
scope.

Prompts are written to standard error, or to standard output with **-s**.

Before the first prompt, the REPL loads the file named by `WATTLE_PROFILE`
into its environment, unless **-R** was given.

ENVIRONMENT
===========

- `WATTLE_PATH`:
  A list of directories to load system modules from, separated by ``:``, or by
  ``;`` on Windows. The last directory becomes ``*syspath*`` and is searched
  first. If unset, ``*syspath*`` is `/usr/local/lib/wattle`.

- `WATTLE_PROFILE`:
  A Janet file to load before the REPL starts. It is not loaded when a
  _script_ runs without **-r**.

- `NO_COLOR`:
  If set to a non-empty value, disable colour. Colour is otherwise used when
  both standard output and standard error are terminals.

An environment variable set to the empty string is treated as unset.

EXIT STATUS
===========

**wattle** exits `0` on success and `1` if a script or an option raises an
error that is not handled. An error in the REPL is printed and does not end the
session. A program can choose its own status with ``(os/exit status)``.

EXAMPLES
========

Start the REPL:

````
$ wattle
````

Run a script with two arguments:

````
$ wattle hello.wattle Alice Bob
````

Evaluate an expression and print its result:

````
$ wattle -e '(print (+ 1 2 3))'
````

Compile a program into an image and run the image:

````
$ wattle -c hello.wattle hello.jimage
$ wattle -i hello.jimage Alice
````

Check a script for compile errors without running it:

````
$ wattle -k hello.wattle
````

SEE ALSO
========

The Janet language is documented at <https://janet-lang.org>.

HISTORY
=======

Wattle is a fork of Janet, the language created by Calvin Rose. Its runtime is
rewritten in Zig, and it has its own version numbering: the binding
``janet/version`` reports the version of Janet it matches.

